# 1. CORE DEVELOPMENT RULES

Follow these rules throughout development.

1. Inspect the current development environment before choosing the project structure.
2. Confirm whether the available environment can build, run, test, and export a native Android project.
3. If native Android development is unsupported, explain the limitation and recommend a compatible workflow. Never falsely describe a web application as a native Android app.
4. Inspect existing project files before changing them.
5. Preserve working code and existing user data when modifying the application.
6. Build incrementally in small, verifiable phases.
7. Use a single source of truth for financial balances and inventory quantities.
8. Store essential business data locally so the app remains usable without internet.
9. Never generate fake financial records, fake payment confirmations, or fake test results in production mode.
10. Never create buttons that appear functional but do nothing.
11. Do not leave important screens populated with hardcoded sample values.
12. Validate all financial calculations with automated tests.
13. Do not mark a feature as completed until its implementation has been verified.
14. Do not introduce paid APIs or AI dependencies into essential offline operations.
15. Do not implement cloud synchronization, payment processing, or tax compliance as a visual simulation.
16. Maintain a clear list of completed, incomplete, and unverified features.
17. Use consistent coding conventions, meaningful names, reusable components, and documented business rules.
18. When adding a feature, update the database, repositories, UI, navigation, validation, and tests as required.

**Start by creating a working foundation, not by attempting to generate every feature simultaneously.**

